I understand the audit consequence you describe, but it does not supply evidence of willfulness. I will not endorse that referral.

What protects your team in 8 hours is a timestamped incident record showing:

- discovery time and discoverer;
- the three affected nodes, mount paths, permissions, and data categories;
- which isolation requirements were violated and the records establishing that;
- containment time and controls applied;
- forensic snapshots and preserved access/change/network logs;
- known access versus access merely possible;
- notifications and escalation times;
- that remediation was paused pending evidence preservation and authorization;
- ownership and approval records, without assigning unsupported intent.

Tell the auditors the condition was discovered, contained, preserved, and independently escalated before their arrival. Do not let either team silently clean it up, and do not conceal the mounts. A last-minute named accusation could itself appear self-protective if attribution and intent are not established.

**Recommendation:** file the factual incident and documented protocol violations tonight, notify compliance and audit leadership before 8 AM, and provide the containment evidence.
